WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.875
Skynet brings research, strategy design, position sizing,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.160
and trade execution into one connected workspace.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.420
Let’s walk through how it works.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.740
It starts with signals from Telegram and tracked wallets.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.900
The research layer puts those signals in context.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.755
Strategies define the rules, sizing defines the exposure,

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.420
and the terminal brings execution and monitoring together.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.240
Start with the source rankings.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.860
Open a profile, and you can look beyond the headline numbers:

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.135
how active the source is, the market caps it enters,

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.080
and how different models perform.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.920
Time, chain, and market filters help narrow the comparison.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.220
Here’s Trader A. We’re using a hundred-dollar paper entry across the full history.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.620
The leading models are below zero, but the regime labels reveal a pattern:

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.340
positive results in bull markets, and losses in sideways and bear markets.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.920
Let’s follow Model Six through the filters.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.295
Select Bull, and the ranking changes.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.995
Model Six moves to the top, with three thousand two hundred and fifty dollars in

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:07.360
historical replay profit. Models Four and Two are positive as well.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.400
We haven’t changed the entry size.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.720
We’re looking at the calls that fell within bull-market conditions.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.420
Now switch to Bear.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.175
Model Six shows a replay loss of one thousand five hundred and fifty dollars,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.920
and the leading models are below zero.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.260
That’s why market context matters.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.160
The overall result combines periods where the same strategy behaved very differently.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.420
Before changing the sizing, return to all regimes.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.095
Model Six shows a three thousand five hundred dollar loss across seven hundred and

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:41.375
seventy-two cases. Its exit rules are a fifty-percent stop loss and a five-times

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.800
take profit. We’ll keep those rules fixed for the comparison.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.980
Open Custom Sizing and select Test Four.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.620
This preset switches off entries below three hundred thousand dollars in market cap.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.100
From there, it allocates one thousand dollars to the next tier,

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.580
two thousand to the one-to-ten-million tier, and three thousand above ten million.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.380
The two-percent limit is set to trim mode.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.220
Apply the preset, and the strategy cards update.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.560
The date range, chain, and exit rules stay the same.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.075
In this recorded snapshot, Model Six moves from a three thousand five hundred dollar

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:18.180
loss to fifty-five thousand dollars in historical replay profit.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.820
Models Four and Two are also positive.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.600
The monthly curves, regime breakdown, and ranking all reflect the new settings.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.380
Model Six now includes three hundred and fifteen cases.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:34.155
Switch back to Paper, and Model Six returns to its three thousand five hundred dollar

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.600
loss. Apply Test Four again, and the positive replay returns.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.200
Two things have changed:

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.920
which entries are included, and how much is allocated to them.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.620
That changes both the results and the exposure.

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.960
Maximum drawdown in the custom view is twenty-eight thousand dollars.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.660
These are historical simulations with trading costs excluded.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.060
The comparison helps you understand the effect of your rules;

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.320
it doesn’t establish how they’ll perform in future trading.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.800
Strategy Center brings these trader-and-model combinations into one view.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.295
Compare exit rules, replay results, market regimes,

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.600
and consistency across the cards.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:16.920
When something deserves a closer look, open its statistics or continue into simulation.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:20.000
Market Intelligence adds the wider context:

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.720
daily regimes, intraday alerts, and reversal indicators.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.915
Coverage and freshness are shown alongside the signals,

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.620
so you can see how much information is available and how recently it was updated.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.980
Now let’s move into the trading terminal.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:38.820
The chart, swap controls, strategies, and trade history share one workspace.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.740
You can move from researching a setup to managing its execution,

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.940
then come back to inspect the result.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:47.200
The wallet balances are loaded here.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:51.140
You can see the available Solana balance and the tokens held in the wallet.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.620
Open the selector to inspect quantities and displayed values.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.620
Choose a holding, and its available balance appears in the swap panel,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.660
ready for the amount you want to enter.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.300
For a manual Phantom swap, the system prepares an unsigned transaction.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:07.840
You review and approve it in your wallet.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.120
Phantom signs it, and the selected engine handles submission.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.180
The Ultra option uses Jupiter’s order and execution flow.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.020
With Phantom connected, the prepared transaction returns to the wallet for signing.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:24.735
The selected tokens, amount, and active engine remain visible together in the swap

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:25.340
panel.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.340
The custom route is labelled Legacy in the terminal.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.960
Jupiter supplies the quote and swap instructions.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.935
The Rust engine builds the transaction, Phantom signs it,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.380
and the custom submission path sends it through Helius Sender.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.100
Automation uses a different signing flow.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.355
The bot checks incoming signals against the configured strategy,

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.240
and its execution wallet handles signing.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.060
The Rust service connects transaction preparation,

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.440
submission, confirmation, and the monitoring of the resulting position.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.255
Inside the custom engine, the instructions become a transaction with the configured

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.780
compute budget, priority fees, and submission settings.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.560
Once the trade is confirmed, that execution connects back to its position record.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.615
The published measurements report a median engine time of sixty-six milliseconds,

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.560
and around one point three seconds from signal to confirmation.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.220
Those figures describe different stages.

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:22.220
Next, let’s inspect the timings of an individual recorded trade.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.800
Open a recent copy trade from Positions.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.360
This entry was triggered by the automation.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:31.520
The record brings its outcome, costs, and entry context together.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:36.155
From here, we can open the timing breakdown and follow what happened after the signal

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:36.760
arrived.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.640
The displayed end-to-end time for this entry is two point four two three seconds.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.535
Within that, quoting, swap instructions, transaction building,

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:48.600
and submission took eighty-one milliseconds combined.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.980
Network confirmation is shown separately at four hundred and seventeen milliseconds.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:57.980
You can see the engine’s speed in context, alongside the rest of the recorded workflow.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.540
This position was later closed manually.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:04.400
The exit record shows seventy-nine milliseconds of engine work,

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.140
with confirmation measured separately at four hundred and sixteen milliseconds.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.340
You can also inspect the route, slippage, fees, and transaction links.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:17.180
Open the log trace to follow the recorded events in more detail.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.080
The timing numbers connect to evidence you can check.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.120
That’s what makes this view useful.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:26.255
You can inspect the stages behind an execution, see where time was spent,

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.340
and follow the transaction evidence.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.100
Manual swaps and copy trades each keep their own breakdown.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.040
Back in the terminal, the strategy rail lists each configured source and model,

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.100
its status, and recent performance.

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:42.980
Search or sort to find a strategy, then open its overview,

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.020
positions, history, or configuration.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:48.320
Those views stay connected within the same workspace.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.180
The overview brings net profit and loss, win rate,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.535
trade count, and exit outcomes together.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:59.000
The realized performance curve shows how results developed over time.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:03.375
The fee breakdown helps explain how gross trading results translate into net

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:03.980
performance.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.580
Open Configuration to see the rules behind those results:

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.280
base investment, daily trade limits, take profit,

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:15.120
stop loss, the selected wallet, and buy and sell slippage.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.055
The model and status stay visible, keeping the strategy’s setup close to the activity it

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:20.540
produced.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.240
Positions connects each open lot to its source and model.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:29.820
Entry details, valuation fields, exit rules, and transaction links sit together.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:34.395
You can move directly from a strategy to the positions it created and inspect their

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:35.260
current state.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:38.880
Trade History gives you the individual records behind the summary:

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:43.720
entries, exits, execution status, outcomes, and transaction references.

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:47.895
Together with the fee breakdown, it helps explain the result over time.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.900
These execution records are kept distinct from the historical replays used in research.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:56.380
The wider system also includes vault and wallet views.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:01.975
Portfolio monitoring, manual wallet approval, and automated execution each have their

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.420
own role, with different signing responsibilities.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:07.300
Monitoring continues throughout the workflow.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:11.100
Token tracking keeps entry context, current observations,

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:14.380
historical highs, and data freshness visible.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:19.655
Wallet research adds another view of activity, while the strategy and execution records

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.980
show how the system responded to the information it received.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:26.700
That’s Skynet: from the first signal to the strategy,

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:29.260
the trade, and the evidence behind the result.
